# Repository Backlog

Backlog uses repository documents only: goals in ARCH-01, milestones under `docs/roadmap/`, queued requests as draft
proposals, and follow-ups as feedback.

## Requests and decomposition

- Classify requests in `direct` mode. Start immediate `session`/`spec` work through inline Planning; keep every
  request not starting now as a draft proposal, including small work.
- Follow the draft's explicit intent gate first. A draft whose direction is settled may be accepted directly (below);
  otherwise Planning runs at its effort.
- Before editing, splitting or deleting a draft, run `devkit spec proposal check <slug>` (fetch first, within
  existing network authorization). A `reviewing` listing is no check.
  - An existing local or fetched `plan-<slug>` means Planning owns the draft: stop on `SPEC_PROPOSAL_IN_PLANNING`
    and leave the base copy unedited.
- Review open feedback. Promote a repository-owned item selected for work through feedback hygiene into a draft,
  optionally assigned a milestone. Preserve its destination and avoid duplicate drafts.
- Decompose into drafts only: `devkit spec proposal new <child> --decomposed-from PROP-<parent>` checks the original
  is an editable draft.
  - Preserve the parent's milestone with `--milestone MS-<slug>` when set.
  - Choose each child's effort hints afresh.
  - Preserve open feedback ([feedback retirement](feedback-retirement.md)) before deleting the parent in the same
    commit.
- Backlog edits and milestone changes are outside Planning/Development worktree and PR requirements.
  - Commit only their agreed files in the current checkout; preserve unrelated changes.
  - Commit a direct acceptance and a reclassification deletion only on the base checkout, once you settled that you
    can push to the base (or that there is no remote). A protected base keeps the draft on Planning.
  - Undo a stranded commit with `git revert`, not a reset.

## Return from Planning for decomposition

When Planning discovers a split is needed, its executor follows [Planning](planning.md): preserve evidence in
`materials/`, set `status: draft` and `needs_decompose: true`, close every open question with an answer recording
that Planning returned to Backlog, and commit the return with the run's records. Do not create children on the
Planning branch or retry `--decomposed-from` there.

The Host then:

1. Check the committed return and establish authority to merge that return and perform cleanup under the
   existing Git policy. Reuse existing authorization for these exact effects instead of adding a routine
   decomposition approval.
2. Merge the return:
   - For a local merge, prepare the branch and clear envelope holds, then run
     `devkit worktree finish plan-<slug> --merge --local-only` from the base.
   - Use the required PR path when
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}policy requires it. The MCP unit integration path requires an accepted proposal and cannot integrate this draft
{% else %}policy requires it. The automated unit integration path requires an accepted proposal and cannot integrate this draft
{% endif %}return. Do not manufacture `plan-accept` to satisfy that readiness check.
   - This is a return of a draft, not `plan-accept` or authorization to implement the old proposal.
3. Preserve the committed return on the base before removing the local Planning branch and worktree.
4. A fetched remote Planning branch also blocks Backlog: coordinate its authorized remote removal and
   refresh/prune the fetched ref under existing network authority. Do not hide a live remote branch by deleting only
   its tracking ref.
5. On the base, run `devkit spec proposal check <slug>` again. Only after it passes may Backlog create the children
   with `--decomposed-from`, preserve source feedback and retire the parent. `status: draft` alone is insufficient
   while a local or fetched Planning branch exists.

If authorization or branch cleanup is pending, keep the return pending and do not decompose. This procedure adds no
merge, push or deletion permission and gives workers no additional Git actions.

## Direct acceptance

An eligible draft may skip Planning under the `plan-accept` policy.

- Apply the eligibility checks before acceptance.
- Skip Planning only when direction is settled and no comparison, spike question or failed-attempt depth escalation
  needs it.
- Preserve an explicit request for Planning.

1. In the base checkout run `devkit spec proposal accept <slug> --by user --direct --check --note "<reason>"`. This
   is a read-only eligibility check, not a user approval. Resolve any failed condition. For a pending
   `gate:plan-intent`, reuse existing explicit task intent instead of prompting again.
2. Before acceptance, provide the eligibility reason, `task_kind`, Planning skipped, PADRs to promote, selected
   `dev_effort`, and any missing PR review under `pull_request = required`.
   - Under `plan-accept=ask`, obtain or reuse explicit direction acceptance.
   - Under `auto`, use policy authority from the owning base and task policy.
3. Sweep this session at `collect-feedback` under the project's sweep policy (`spec-feedback`; zero observations is
   a valid PASS).
4. Run `devkit spec proposal accept <slug> --by user|policy --direct --note "<reason>"` with the actual authority and
   commit the agreed Backlog change. No Planning run, `activity-effort` or `worker` envelope.
   - If the acceptance is refused after the sweep, remove that sweep envelope before committing.

- Development follows the independent `proposal-handoff=ask|auto` boundary; direction acceptance alone does not imply
  a human decision to start Development.
- `accept_only` can retain the accepted proposal in Backlog.
- For `SPEC_PROPOSAL_ACCEPTANCE_UNRECORDED`, inspect the exact state. Repair a missing direct acceptance only with
  valid authority and eligibility; never invent approval to satisfy handoff.

## Session reclassification of a queued draft

When the user picks a queued draft whose work meets the `session` criteria ([classification](classification.md) §1),
the Host may choose a `session` task within the authorized goal, scope and constraints, stating the reason.

- A worker or Cloud Agent returns a recommendation instead of mutating Backlog.
- Respect any explicit requirement to retain the proposal or Planning.
- Require push access to the base, a passing `devkit spec proposal check <slug>` (fetch first), no `PADR-*.md` in
  `decisions/`, and no `needs_decompose`, `supersedes` or depth escalation.
- The request names what goes: the draft and its gate records, its materials (kept in Git history), its `milestone`
  membership (review that milestone's exit condition) and the open feedback preserved.

1. Delete the draft in one Backlog commit on the base, preserving open feedback and striking through ordinary prose
   references ([feedback retirement](feedback-retirement.md)).
2. In the same commit, disposition direct prerequisites as below.
3. Use the commit message "depth proposal -> session on the user's decision: <reason>" and name the commit to the
   user at once.
4. The session takes a new slug, starts through inline Planning and names the deletion commit in its final report.
5. If the session never integrates, undo the deletion by `git revert` of that commit, under existing Git authority.

## Direct prerequisites and lifecycle disposition

- For explicit proposal pickup and direct-spec intake, apply `spec-next/references/next-work.md`'s direct
  prerequisite procedure.
- Author optional `depends_on: [PROP-foundation, SPEC-parser]` frontmatter; omit it when empty.
- Unit and MS dependencies stay separate. Order overrides never waive structural errors.

Before splitting/decomposing, superseding, rejecting, deleting or reclassifying to session:

1. Query `devkit spec refs PROP-<slug> --direction in -n 0` (refs is repository-wide) and inspect depends_on on all
   live incoming unit anchors. Completed historical anchors remain unchanged.
2. Review each edge with the user: replace it with the required child/successor set, or remove it
   only when the prerequisite is explicitly waived.
3. Commit that per-dependent disposition in the same authorized change.

- A split can require both children or only one per dependent; never infer successors from `decomposed_from` or
  `supersedes`.
- Rejection and deletion do not fulfill an edge.
- A retained rejected PROP remains resolvable but blocked; an unresolved missing target is invalid, never ready.
- If a required dependent edit lies outside the allowed write scope, stop the lifecycle mutation and ask the Host to
  coordinate scope. Do not silently delete the prerequisite or mutate another worker's checkout.
- Exact same-slug handoff relocation remains automatic for live direct references and carries outgoing
  dependencies, after a projected-graph preflight before writes. It implies no fulfillment.

## Coarse milestone authoring

A milestone SHOULD describe a multi-unit phase, release or architectural theme advancing ARCH-01. Authors SHOULD
NOT create a milestone solely as a single-proposal sequencing gate or split a cohesive theme merely for ordering;
use unit depends_on instead. A theme may initially contain one proposal while additional scope is not authored,
provided its intended outcome is multi-unit. This is guidance, not a minimum-member-count validator.

Exit conditions SHOULD state observable phase-level outcomes and capability boundaries. CLI flags, branch names,
file layouts and scenario-level tests belong in proposals/specs. Existing milestone transition authority and
MS readiness semantics remain unchanged.

## Milestone documents and user decisions

Use `.devkit/managed/spec/templates/milestone.md` for a global `docs/roadmap/MS-<slug>.md` document. Slugs start with a
word, for example MS-release-1-0. Required metadata is id, title, English summary, status and created_at; optional
`depends_on` lists milestone IDs. Body: the goal it advances (cite ARCH-01), exit condition and non-goals. Do not
duplicate work lists: only direct PROP anchors own the optional `milestone` field; use `refs --direction in`.
User and agent jointly review the theme and decompose it into proposals. Handoff MUST NOT carry milestone metadata
to SPEC; historical SPEC metadata is ignored for membership. Use code/Git inspection on demand for downstream
outcomes, never a persistent MS-to-PROP-to-SPEC lineage graph.

Status is planned, active, done or dropped; several milestones may be active. A milestone is ready when all
dependencies are done or dropped. Cycles are rule errors; the user may activate one out of order.

Actual semantic scope changes (new required work, exclusion or moving scope elsewhere) reset both Completion
review assertions to pending. Normal handoff or mechanical identity relocation does not change scope and requires
no milestone review/reset. Follow the close procedure for changed evidence and user-owned reopening.

Every transition is the user's decision, with a concrete candidate prepared first:

1. Activate: show each dependency not done/dropped; the user may still activate it.
2. Close planned/active: follow [milestone completion](milestone-completion.md), including relevant known
   `plan-`/`dev-` activity. Confirm both assertions and done together.
3. Drop: show unsettled members and dependent successor milestones. Prepare member reassignment, clearing milestone
   or proposal rejection; show each successor so the user keeps or changes its dependencies.

Hygiene reads base facts only: `SPEC_MILESTONE_DONE_UNSETTLED` and `SPEC_MILESTONE_DROPPED_UNSETTLED` name unsettled
direct proposal members left on finished milestones until reassigned/cleared/rejected or handed off; handoff
removes membership without certifying milestone completion.

